{
  "requirement": "The SDK exposes a source-provider interface for enumerating resource identities\nwithin a requested scope and reading resource bytes. Resources need no local\nfilesystem path.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed test adequately checks `requirements.sdk.sources.provider`.\n\nThe requirement has three obligations. The SDK must expose a source-provider interface that enumerates resource identities within a requested scope. The same interface must read resource bytes. Resources must not need a local filesystem path.\n\n- **Interface exists and is implementable.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/sources.rs#impl[Memory/SourceProvider]` implements the public `SourceProvider` trait with only `list(&SourceScope)` and `read(&ResourceId)`. That implementation compiles against the trait, so the trait exposes both operations.\n- **Reading bytes.** `fn[defaults_use_provider_primitives_and_keep_one_parse_snapshot]` calls `read` directly. It asserts that the returned `SourceFile` bytes decode to `version 1...`. `parse` and `search_text` also read through the provider. The test asserts both the read count and the event order `[\"src/one.txt\",\"src/one.txt\",\"list\",\"src/one.txt\"]`.\n- **Enumerating within a scope.** `search_text` is called with `SourceScope { prefix: \"src\" }`. The test asserts that `list` was invoked. `Memory::list` returns both `src/one.txt` and `other/two.txt`. The test then asserts that only `src/one.txt` is read afterwards and that exactly 2 matches come back. That shows enumerated identities are used and restricted to the requested scope.\n- **No local filesystem path.** `Memory` uses `ResourceId { scheme: \"memory\", location }` and holds its bytes in memory. The test never touches the filesystem, and the whole flow works on a provider with no path.\n- **Failure behavior.** `fn[failed_provider_operations_never_become_successful_empty_results]` is marked for `provider.errors` rather than this subject. It also confirms that errors from `list` and `read` propagate through the interface.\n\nThe requirement is a structural interface requirement, and I found no distinct input classes or modes that the test misses. The test is marked `{test}` for the subject. I found no gaps.",
  "references": "c092e65ea56c80be4b66bb5dab000c633b5e10a5e17ccdb33d90491602a26904",
